WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.720
Email that works for you, and phishing that does not fool you.

2
00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:09.430
This is the video for module two: email. Set it up, use it well, spot the traps.

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.990
Schools, employers, banks and online courses send official messages by email.

4
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:23.130
Gmail is free. One Google account opens Gmail, the Play Store, Drive and Photos.

5
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:25.980
Clear emails get answers.

6
00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.870
And you need to spot phishing before it costs you money.

7
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:40.040
Step one. Choose an address you would put on a CV, like amina dot bello at gmail dot com. Avoid nicknames and random numbers.

8
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:47.600
Step two. Enter your first name and surname as they appear on your ID, because some services check this.

9
00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:52.590
Step three. Create a strong password of at least 8 characters.

10
00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:04.890
Step four. Add your own phone number and a recovery email. Most people lose email by forgetting the password or losing the phone. Recovery details are your spare key.

11
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:13.030
Step five. Open myaccount dot google dot com, tap Security Checkup, and follow each suggestion.

12
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:21.800
Never let a phone seller or business centre create your account with their number. Whoever controls the recovery number can take the account.

13
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:26.420
When you tap Compose, put the main person in the To box.

14
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.320
Cc is for people who should know, but do not need to act.

15
00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:41.380
When you email many customers who do not know each other, use Bcc. It protects their privacy and stops reply-all reaching everyone.

16
00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:49.950
Tap the paperclip to attach a file, and give it a clear name. Files above 25 MB go as a Drive link.

17
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:59.160
Swipe to archive, star what needs action, and search. Kemi in Ibadan found a customer receipt in under a minute this way.

18
00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:09.300
A busy reader decides in seconds. So the subject says what the email is about and what you need. Never leave it blank or write only hello.

19
00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:15.230
Greet the reader properly. If you do not know the name, write Dear Admissions Team.

20
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.190
Put your purpose in the first line, so they do not have to search for it.

21
00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.450
Give the details in short paragraphs, and mention every attachment.

22
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:33.740
End with a clear next step, then Kind regards, your name and phone number. Avoid text spelling like pls.

23
00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:40.720
Email can be kept forever, so never write your BVN, card details, PIN or password in one.

24
00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:48.840
Rukayat in Ilorin first wrote: hello sir pls I need my money. No subject, no details.

25
00:02:50.090 --> 00:03:04.730
She rewrote it. The subject says, invoice for 20 aprons delivered 12 May. She opens with Dear Mr Adeyemi, and gives the agreed price of 4,500 naira each, a total of 90,000 naira.

26
00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.410
She asks for the transfer by Friday, and now has a dated written record.

27
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:21.730
Phishing is a message that pretends to come from someone you trust, to steal your password, OTP, card details or money. By SMS it is called smishing.

28
00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:32.820
Watch for fear and urgency, prizes you did not expect, and requests for secrets. Real banks do not ask for these by email, SMS or call.

29
00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:42.850
The bold name in Gmail is only a display name. Tap it to see the real address. Zenith Bank Security sending from a Gmail address is a scam.

30
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:56.310
Esther in Abuja got an SMS asking for a 1,250 naira customs fee to release her parcel. First, she paused, and read the link. It was a strange domain.

31
00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:06.840
Then she used the tracking link in the shop's own email. No fee was due. If a friend's WhatsApp asks for money, call them on a normal phone call first.

32
00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:22.170
If you already typed something, act at once. Change the password and turn on two-step verification. If you shared bank details or an OTP, call the number on your card. Speed matters more than embarrassment.

33
00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:31.610
To remember. Add your recovery details on day one. Use Bcc for groups. Put your message in the subject and first line.

34
00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:39.430
Tap the display name to see who really sent it. And when a message rushes you, pause and check through a channel you already trust.

35
00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:51.190
Now practise. Check your recovery details, tidy your inbox, write one real email with a signature, and find two suspicious messages and their warning signs.

36
00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.480
Continue with the lessons below.
